1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:10,500
Two, three, five, seven, eleven, thirteen. The prime numbers. One at a time, they look random.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:17,900
But count them, and they follow a smooth curve, with a small wobble. How big can that wobble get?

3
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:23,100
The answer is hidden in the zeros of one function.

4
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:32,667
In September 2026, OpenAI released a paper claiming to keep those zeros away from the edge, for the first time.

5
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:40,233
It is not the Riemann hypothesis. But if it holds, it is a major step toward it.

6
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:58,234
A prime is a whole number bigger than one that only divides by one and itself. There is no simple rule for the next one.

7
00:00:58,234 --> 00:01:07,167
Here is a staircase that climbs one step at every prime. Up to one hundred, there are twenty-five of them.

8
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:11,767
And here is a smooth curve that follows the staircase closely.

9
00:01:11,767 --> 00:01:21,734
That the count of primes follows this curve forever is the prime number theorem, proved in 1896.

10
00:01:21,734 --> 00:01:25,234
The interesting part is the gap between the two.

11
00:01:25,234 --> 00:01:34,067
Up to a million there are seventy-eight thousand primes, and the curve is off by only about a hundred and thirty.

12
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:42,467
How large can that gap ever get? Nobody knows exactly. But we know what decides it.

13
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:52,500
So: primes follow a smooth curve, and the question is the size of the wobble around it.

14
00:01:56,733 --> 00:02:03,000
In 1859, Bernhard Riemann studied one function, called zeta.

15
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,767
Add up one, plus one over two to the s, plus one over three to the s, and so on forever.

16
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:22,833
The input s is a complex number. Think of it as a point on a map, with a position from left to right and a height.

17
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:29,767
Euler had noticed that this sum over all whole numbers equals a product over the primes alone.

18
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:35,600
So zeta knows about every prime at once.

19
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:48,100
Riemann found an exact formula for the prime count: the smooth curve, plus one wave for each place where zeta equals zero.

20
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:53,033
How loud each wave is depends on how far right its zero sits.

21
00:02:53,033 --> 00:02:58,767
A zero near position one makes a wave almost as big as the count itself.

22
00:02:58,767 --> 00:03:07,600
A zero at one half makes the smallest wave possible, about the square root of x.

23
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:14,733
All the interesting zeros live in a band between positions zero and one, called the critical strip.

24
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:18,600
Here are the first thirteen.

25
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:27,067
They come in mirror pairs: a zero at position beta comes with one at one minus beta.

26
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:38,300
So the zeros of zeta set the wobble of the primes, and the further right a zero sits, the louder its wave.

27
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:49,167
The Riemann hypothesis says every zero in the strip sits exactly on the line at one half.

28
00:03:49,167 --> 00:03:56,633
Computers have checked more than ten trillion of them, and every one is on the line.

29
00:03:56,633 --> 00:04:04,100
It is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, and still unproved.

30
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:12,667
What has been proved is far weaker. In 1896 it was shown that there are no zeros on the right edge itself.

31
00:04:12,667 --> 00:04:17,267
That was enough for the prime number theorem.

32
00:04:17,267 --> 00:04:25,200
Later work pushed the zeros a little way in from the edge, but only by a margin that shrinks as you go higher up.

33
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:32,533
Far enough up, the zeros might creep right up to the edge.

34
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:37,000
So mathematicians asked a much more modest question.

35
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,800
Is there some fixed line, anywhere short of the edge, with no zeros to its right, at any height at all?

36
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:54,100
This is the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. Nobody could prove it with any number.

37
00:04:54,100 --> 00:05:00,567
The same questions apply to a family of cousins of zeta, called Dirichlet L-functions.

38
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:09,533
Each one counts primes in a pattern, like the primes that leave remainder one when divided by four.

39
00:05:09,533 --> 00:05:18,000
For these, there was one more worry: a single rogue zero, sitting on the real line extremely close to one.

40
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,067
It is called a Landau–Siegel zero, and nobody could rule it out.

41
00:05:25,067 --> 00:05:35,733
So the question was: is there some fixed line, short of the edge, with no zeros to its right?

42
00:05:39,966 --> 00:05:51,166
Here is the claim. Zeta, and every one of its Dirichlet cousins, has no zeros with position greater than seven eighths.

43
00:05:51,166 --> 00:05:58,066
No exceptions: at every height, for every pattern of primes.

44
00:05:58,066 --> 00:06:02,966
By the mirror symmetry, there are none to the left of one eighth either.

45
00:06:02,966 --> 00:06:08,866
Every zero is trapped in the middle three quarters of the strip.

46
00:06:08,866 --> 00:06:14,466
And the rogue zero near one is gone with them.

47
00:06:14,466 --> 00:06:22,466
The paper proves this in two stages: first a line at eleven twelfths, then sharpened to seven eighths.

48
00:06:22,466 --> 00:06:32,100
A shorter companion paper, written with human help, gives a second proof of the eleven twelfths line.

49
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:44,100
So the claim is: no zeros past seven eighths, for zeta and all its cousins. Now, how is it proved?

50
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:52,467
Give every whole number a score from its prime factors.

51
00:06:52,467 --> 00:07:04,167
An even number of different primes scores plus one, an odd number scores minus one, and a repeated prime factor scores zero.

52
00:07:04,167 --> 00:07:11,967
These scores behave like coin flips. Add them up, and the running total stays small.

53
00:07:11,967 --> 00:07:22,000
This total is tied exactly to the zeros. A zero at position beta would push it up to about x to the beta.

54
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,900
And the other way round: if you can prove the total never grows faster than x to some power theta,

55
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:34,067
there are no zeros to the right of theta.

56
00:07:34,067 --> 00:07:39,100
So the proof assumes there is a rightmost zero beyond the line,

57
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:47,033
builds one sum in two ways, and shows it would have to be both large and small.

58
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:56,467
So: no zeros past a line is the same as sums that provably cancel.

59
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:07,234
Bounding one such sum directly is hopeless. That is the whole difficulty.

60
00:08:07,234 --> 00:08:16,867
So the proof hides it in a crowd. It builds a large family of related sums, by twisting the original with extra symbols,

61
00:08:16,867 --> 00:08:22,234
cousins of asking whether a number is a perfect square.

62
00:08:22,234 --> 00:08:30,200
Two facts do the work. First, the family as a whole cancels well on average.

63
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:40,667
Second, our sum appears in the family many times over, as nearly identical copies, one for each of many primes.

64
00:08:40,667 --> 00:08:47,534
If our sum were large, its copies alone would push the average over the bound.

65
00:08:47,534 --> 00:08:51,934
So it must be small.

66
00:08:51,934 --> 00:08:58,867
To bound the average, the proof rewrites it and finds special sums with a hidden symmetry.

67
00:08:58,867 --> 00:09:08,100
They are the coefficients of the cubic theta function, studied by Kubota and Patterson since the nineteen seventies.

68
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:18,534
That symmetry trades a long, hard sum for a shorter one. Repeating the trade shrinks the problem, step by step.

69
00:09:18,534 --> 00:09:24,334
All of this happens in a different number system: the Eisenstein integers,

70
00:09:24,334 --> 00:09:31,834
built from a cube root of one, laid out on a grid of triangles.

71
00:09:31,834 --> 00:09:37,367
Each Dirichlet L-function is a factor of an L-function of this number system.

72
00:09:37,367 --> 00:09:47,167
So a zero-free line proved for the whole Eisenstein family carries back to zeta and its cousins.

73
00:09:47,167 --> 00:09:50,434
The first stage reaches eleven twelfths.

74
00:09:50,434 --> 00:10:01,800
The second changes the setup, with sums of unequal lengths and carefully chosen primes, and squeezes the line to seven eighths.

75
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:13,067
So: hide the sum in a crowd, bound the crowd with a hidden symmetry, and carry the result back to zeta.

76
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:27,200
If it holds up, several old problems fall at once. The rogue zeros near one are gone for good.

77
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:32,334
For every prime, the smallest number that is not a perfect square,

78
00:10:32,334 --> 00:10:39,600
after dividing by that prime, is tiny: at most a fixed power of the logarithm of the prime.

79
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:45,634
That settles an old conjecture of Vinogradov.

80
00:10:45,634 --> 00:10:54,067
It means square roots modulo a prime can be found quickly, without random guessing.

81
00:10:54,067 --> 00:11:02,600
Primes in every pattern follow their smooth curves, with a guaranteed bound on the error.

82
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:14,167
And the companion paper notes it confirms that Euler's list of sixty-five special numbers, called idoneal numbers, is complete.

83
00:11:14,167 --> 00:11:21,800
One zero-free line, and several old results follow.

84
00:11:26,033 --> 00:11:30,767
OpenAI also published a computer-checked version in Lean.

85
00:11:30,767 --> 00:11:40,500
The statement for zeta is one line: if the real part of s is more than seven eighths, zeta of s is not zero.

86
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:43,333
We have not re-run it ourselves.

87
00:11:43,333 --> 00:11:52,733
And a computer check certifies the theorem, not the hundred and ninety-nine page written argument.

88
00:11:52,733 --> 00:12:00,433
People are now reading it. Quanta Magazine reported in October that number theorists are working through the proof,

89
00:12:00,433 --> 00:12:07,833
and that Peter Sarnak turned a course he teaches into a study of it.

90
00:12:07,833 --> 00:12:12,467
Others have already rewritten the related argument about rogue zeros:

91
00:12:12,467 --> 00:12:22,267
William Banks with explicit numbers, Bryce Kerr with a simpler proof, and Ben Green with his own account.

92
00:12:22,267 --> 00:12:29,667
No error has been reported so far. No journal has reviewed it yet.

93
00:12:29,667 --> 00:12:38,867
So: a computer-checked statement, and a human reading that is still under way.

94
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:49,867
It does not prove the Riemann hypothesis. That needs every zero on the line at one half.

95
00:12:49,867 --> 00:12:56,534
This keeps them between one eighth and seven eighths.

96
00:12:56,534 --> 00:13:08,000
The wobble it guarantees is about x to the seven eighths, still far from the square root of x the Riemann hypothesis predicts.

97
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:14,967
And experts doubt this method can be pushed all the way to one half.

98
00:13:14,967 --> 00:13:18,434
The primes still keep their deepest secret.

99
00:13:18,434 --> 00:13:26,900
But if this proof holds, their zeros have finally been pushed away from the edge.
